  <div>
    <h4 class="software-title">My Software Development Portfolio</h4>
    <hr class="dotted-line">
    <div class="software-content">
      <ul>
        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li class="pastproj-subtitle"><h4>Stock Screening Application</h4></li>
          <img src="images/sw-Financedemo2.png" alt="" width="100%">
          <p> I independently developed this desktop application in C++ using the Qt framework starting 
              in 2016 to strengthen my C++ engineering skills and explore quantitative analysis of financial markets.
              The system was continuously improved over five years during personal time and demonstrates 
              several capabilities including chart visualization, technical indicator analysis, and 
              experimentation with simple quantitative trading strategies. The application includes automated 
              data ingestion of publicly available historical market data from sources such as SGX and 
              Yahoo Finance, enabling large-scale time-series analysis and backtesting.
              It supports backtesting across 38 technical indicators, allowing experimentation with parameter 
              configurations and evaluation of potential trading strategies.
              A short video demonstration of the application is available
              <a href="https://1drv.ms/v/c/d893771471b95466/IQDR80LGyEpMT46fj4IzHxcrAWNcNDyTNAyYCFSw5Z1uZD4?e=xySwZF"><b>here</b></a>.
            <!-- <a href="https://1drv.ms/v/s!AmZUuXEUd5PYjI9hlCPTD6M26niPnw?e=MvBYAE"><b>here</b></a>. -->
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li class="pastproj-subtitle"><h4>Stock Price Prediction using Machine Learning</h4></li>
          <p> To further develop my Python and machine learning skills, I built a prototype system to 
              explore stock price prediction using historical data collected from the stock screening application.
              The project utilizes modern machine learning frameworks including TensorFlow, Keras, and 
              Scikit-learn to experiment with regression, classification, and LSTM models for time-series 
              forecasting. Data preprocessing, cleaning, and visualization were performed using NumPy, 
              pandas, Matplotlib, and Seaborn.
              This project was designed as an exploratory study into the challenges of financial 
              forecasting. Stock prices are influenced by numerous factors—including economic indicators, 
              company performance, geopolitical events, and market sentiment—which makes accurate prediction 
              inherently difficult. The goal of this work was to investigate how machine learning techniques 
              can be applied to financial time-series data and to better understand the limitations of such models.
              A video demonstration of the project is available via the OneDrive link 
              <a href="https://1drv.ms/v/s!AmZUuXEUd5PYjJ5a2i8zYDrv4S1Ktg?e=GlcN7O"><b>here</b></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li class="pastproj-subtitle"><h4>Application for Upper Limb Exoskeleton</h4></li>
          <img src="images/sw-ULExoGUI_2.png" alt="" width="100%">
          <p> I was responsible for designing the overall software architecture, integrating software components using the 
            Boost library and multithreading, and developing a comprehensive user interface with C++ and Python on Ubuntu 
            using ROS (Robot Operating System) within a six-month timeframe. The objective was to enable real-time data 
            visualization for IMUs, encoders, and force sensors, allowing users to monitor the operational condition of 
            the exoskeleton. The system also supports customized parameter input, with user-defined data stored for 
            personalization, and provides control capabilities for the upper limb exoskeleton. A demonstration of the 
            system can be viewed via the OneDrive link provided
            <a href="https://1drv.ms/v/s!AmZUuXEUd5PYjJ5xuqneegELDF_Egg?e=4dkP9b"><b>here</b></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li class="pastproj-subtitle"><h4>Application for Lower Limb Exoskeleton</h4></li>
          <img src="images/sw-LLExoGUI.gif" alt="" width="100%">
          <p>
            I developed a web-based GUI on Ubuntu using rospy, Plotly Dash, and Flask. The interface enables real-time data 
            visualization for IMUs, encoders, and force sensors integrated into a lower limb exoskeleton. It also provides 
            functionality for controlling the exoskeleton and monitoring its operational status. The plots dynamically adapt 
            to changes in window size to ensure responsive visualization. The data currently displayed are randomly generated 
            placeholders for demonstration purposes. The control panel remains partially incomplete due to my transition to a 
            new company, which limited the time available to fully finalize the project.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li class="pastproj-subtitle"><h4>User Interface for Wheelchair Teleoperation</h4></li>
          <img src="images/sw-MRCA.png" alt="" width="100%">
          <p> I independently developed a web-based user interface within three months to teleoperate an electric wheelchair 
            over a WLAN. Built with JavaScript, HTML, and CSS on Ubuntu using ROS, the interface displays real-time camera 
            feeds and a 2D map of the environment. User registration and login authentication were implemented with MongoDB 
            and Mongoose to control website access. The system leverages state-of-the-art libraries, including rosbridge and 
            roslibjs. A video demonstration is available via the OneDrive link provided
            <a href="https://1drv.ms/v/c/d893771471b95466/IQC6IynL4VgoSKF7tUf_BeeaAarDawWyKZlJsKZq0PXHe6g?e=bQSmSy"><b>here</b></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li class="pastproj-subtitle"><h4>Simulation of Upper Limb Exoskeleton</h4></li>
          <p>
            This robot arm (Upper Limb Exoskeleton) is designed to provide assistive force for patients suffering from stroke,
            spinal cord injuries or weak motor control in their arms to regain the ability to perform daily tasks such as
            eating, drinking, and other personal care activities independently. The assistive support force from the robot arm
            encourages patients to engage more in daily activities, which can contribute to overall physical and mental well-being.
            In other words, improving the ability to perform ADLs also directly impacts a patient's quality of life.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><h4>RViz</h4></li>
            <img src="images/rviz2.gif" alt="" width="100%">
            <p>
                The robot arm is attached to the user's forearm to provide assistive force. Occasionally, collisions occur between
                humans and the robot arm at specific
                postures due to its design. A colleague has developed an algorithm to define a spectrum of postures that could
                potentially lead to collisions. This algorithm determines the angles and positions of both the human arm and the
                robot arm during collisions. I have created a simulation of the human and robot arms in RViz to verify potential
                collisions at these specific postures. Sliding bars are used to adjust the angle of each joint according to the
                orientations and positions of the arms provided by the algorithm, allowing us to visually inspect whether
                collisions occur. Once all potential collisions have been validated, we will improve our control algorithm to
                prevent users of the robot arm from moving into postures that lead to collisions.
            </p>
            <img src="images/CollisionAndNonCollision.png" alt="" width="100%">
            <p>
                The image on the left shows the normal interaction between human and the robot arm. The image on the right shows
                one of the postures detected by our algorithm that leads to a collision. The upper arm (Orange) collides with one
                of the links (Turquoise).
            </p>
            <li><h4>Gazebo</h4></li>
              <img src="images/gazebo.gif" alt="" width="100%">
              <p>
                Another objective of the simulation is to validate the feasibility and capabilities of our developed models.
                The robot arm operates in multiple modes, including compensating for its own gravitational force to maintain
                its posture in 3D space and providing patients with dynamic assistive forces for activities of daily living
                such as eating, drinking, and reaching items. Gazebo can simulate a physics environment; however, due to the
                design of the robotic arm, it encounters difficulties in simulating closed kinematic chains when using URDF.
                The simulation does not enforce constraints to maintain closed linkages, causing the links forming the robot
                arm to separate as soon as the simulation starts. I couldn't switch to SDF as several ROS tools still rely on
                URDF. Therefore, Mujoco is being considered as an alternative option.
              </p>
            <li><h4>Mujoco</h4></li>
              <p>
                Mujoco has the capability to simulate the model in a physics environment. Although Mujoco cannot directly simulate
                closed-loop link mechanisms, it offers unique functions to achieve this. I used its Weld function to create
                closed-loop mechanisms. Subsequently, we employed our developed control models in the simulation for verification.
                Behaviorally, we observed minor differences between the simulation and reality of the robot arm, likely due
                to factors such as unmodeled wires affecting the center of gravity of the robot links and friction in each
                joint. By adjusting parameters such as the friction coefficient, damping, etc., its behavior in the simulation
                aligns more closely with our expectations.
              </p>
              <img src="images/Mujoco_GP.gif" alt="" width="100%">
              <p>
                This image illustrates the robot arm performing the function of gravity compensation. It supports its own weight
                and maintains its posture in 3D space.
              </p>
              <img src="images/Mujoco_RobotHuman.gif" alt="" width="100%">
              <p>
                This image illustrates the robot arm attached to the user's forearm, demonstrating realistic interactions
                in a physical environment.
              </p>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </ul>
